================================================================================
## Explorer expand fails on Databricks SQL (ORDER BY MIN(hop))
================================================================================

Context:
On a Delta (Lakehouse) graph backend every Graph Explorer expand failed in
under a second, at any depth, with MISSING_ATTRIBUTES.RESOLVED_ATTRIBUTE_
APPEAR_IN_OPERATION ("hop" missing). The route maps the error to HTTP 502,
which the explorer shows as "Graph expansion exceeded gateway time limit",
so it looked like a timeout. Cause: ranked_entity_ctes() (added on
2026-10-20) wrote "SELECT entity, MIN(hop) AS hop ... GROUP BY entity
ORDER BY MIN(hop)". The alias shadows the column, so Databricks SQL resolves
the ORDER BY to MIN(MIN(hop)) and rejects it. Postgres (Lakebase) and the
SQLite test accept the same SQL, which is why it was not caught.

Changes:

1. src/back/core/graphdb/adjacency.py
   entity_probe orders by the hop alias (ORDER BY hop, entity) instead of
   ORDER BY MIN(hop). Same order, valid on Databricks SQL, Postgres and
   SQLite. Docstring notes why.
2. tests/units/graphdb/test_adjacency_sql.py
   The hop-ordering shape test expects ORDER BY hop; new test asserts the
   generated SQL never orders by MIN(hop).

Modified files:
- src/back/core/graphdb/adjacency.py
- tests/units/graphdb/test_adjacency_sql.py
- changelogs/v0.9.0/simyung-yang_2026-10-27.log

Tests:
- tests/units/graphdb/test_adjacency_sql.py + delta/test_delta_flat_store.py:
  49 passed (the two hop tests failed before the fix).
- Live check on a SQL warehouse: the old entity_probe CTE fails with the
  same error; the generated expand SQL now succeeds against a real Delta
  graph (depth 1: 17 entities / 209 triples; depth 2: 50 entities capped /
  468 triples).
- Full suite: uv run --frozen pytest -q -m "not scenario" -> 9651 passed,
  338 skipped, 7 deselected, 1 xfailed, 0 failed (14m45s).

