================================================================================
## Lakebase graph engine rejects a bare branch name ("production")
================================================================================

Context:
The deployment docs and deploy.config.sh configure LAKEBASE_BRANCH as a bare
branch id ("production"). The Settings > Lakebase Connection tab pre-fills an
empty branch field from that env var, so saving before the branch picker
loads (or saving through the Advanced JSON) stores
graph_engine_config.lakebase.lakebase_branch = "production" next to
lakebase_project = "projects/<p>". GraphDBFactory, the Lakebase health check
and the graph DB setup path passed that value straight to
BranchLakebaseAuth, which expects "projects/<p>/branches/<b>", so the health
check showed "Health check failed" and the graph backend was unavailable.
Seen on two fresh deployments; the same code exists in 0.8.1.

Changes:

1. src/back/core/graphdb/engine_config.py
   New resolve_lakebase_branch_path(): returns a full branch path; a bare
   branch is joined with lakebase_project ("projects/" prefix added when the
   project is a bare id); unchanged when no project is set.
2. src/back/core/graphdb/GraphDBFactory.py
   Lakebase backend resolves the branch through resolve_lakebase_branch_path.
3. src/back/objects/domain/settings/graphengine/GraphEngineLakebaseSettings.py
   Health check and the graph DB setup / objects / permissions auth path
   resolve the branch (form value or saved value) the same way.
4. tests/units/graphdb/test_graph_engine_config_keys.py
   Tests for full path, bare branch + project path, bare branch + bare
   project id, bare branch without project, and unset branch.

Modified files:
- src/back/core/graphdb/engine_config.py
- src/back/core/graphdb/GraphDBFactory.py
- src/back/objects/domain/settings/graphengine/GraphEngineLakebaseSettings.py
- tests/units/graphdb/test_graph_engine_config_keys.py
- changelogs/v0.9.0/simyung-yang_2026-10-27.log

Test result:
uv run --frozen pytest -q tests/units -k "lakebase or graph_engine or settings"
-> 1741 passed; the only 2 failures are tests/units/settings/
test_deploy_instance_id.py, which depend on uncommitted
deployment-specific deploy.config.sh values. Deployed before the full-suite run finished at the
user's request; the full-suite result is reported separately.

Follow-up: the first full-suite run flagged tests/units/architecture/
test_size_budget.py (GraphEngineLakebaseSettings and two already-oversized
functions grew by a few lines). The resolver import moved to module level in
GraphDBFactory.py and GraphEngineLakebaseSettings.py and the call sites were
rewritten without adding lines, so the frozen size baselines hold.
uv run --frozen pytest -q tests/units -k "architecture or lakebase or
graph_engine or settings" (deploy_instance_id deselected) -> 1751 passed.

================================================================================
## Lakebase managed sync fails on Autoscaling projects ("Database instance is not found")
================================================================================

Context:
SyncedTableManager registered synced tables only through the legacy
/api/2.0/database/synced_tables endpoint with database_instance_name set to
the project id. That endpoint resolves database *instances*; a Lakebase
Autoscaling project created through the Postgres API is not one, so every
managed_synced build failed at "Registering synced table in Unity Catalog"
with "Database instance is not found". Autoscaling projects register synced
tables under /api/2.0/postgres/synced_tables (spec.branch =
projects/<p>/branches/<b>, spec.postgres_database). Verified live: the
Postgres API accepts a regular UC catalog for the synced table and a VIEW as
the source, needs no new_pipeline_spec, and returns the same detailed_state
values (SYNCED_TABLE_*) and pipeline_id the manager already polls.

Changes:

1. src/back/core/graphdb/lakebase/SyncedTableManager.py
   New _RoutedSyncedTablesAPI (extends _RestDatabaseAPI), used by
   _CompatWorkspaceClient:
   - create: POST /api/2.0/postgres/synced_tables?synced_table_id=<fqn> with
     the branch path and Postgres database built from the existing payload;
     falls back to the legacy endpoint only when the Postgres API answers
     not-found (project unknown). "already exists" / ghost-reservation
     errors propagate unchanged to the existing recovery logic.
   - get: Postgres API first, then the legacy endpoint, so tables registered
     the old way keep resolving.
   - delete: removes the registration from both APIs (not-found ignored).
   - Responses are reshaped to the legacy SyncedDatabaseTable form
     (name = UC FQN, data_synchronization_status = status) so ensure /
     trigger / wait / state extraction are unchanged.
2. tests/units/core/test_synced_tables_rest_api.py
   Create payload and query, legacy-shaped response, legacy fallback on an
   unknown project, no fallback on "already exists", get via either API,
   delete from both.

Modified files:
- src/back/core/graphdb/lakebase/SyncedTableManager.py
- tests/units/core/test_synced_tables_rest_api.py
- changelogs/v0.9.0/simyung-yang_2026-10-27.log

Test result:
uv run --frozen pytest -q tests/units/core/test_synced_tables_rest_api.py
tests/units/core/test_synced_table_manager.py tests/units/architecture
-> 42 passed. Live check on a Lakebase Autoscaling project: ensure() created
the synced table through the Postgres API, a probe table reached
ONLINE_NO_PENDING_UPDATE with all rows in Postgres, and delete() removed
both probes (probe objects cleaned up).
